\begin_layout Standard
I told him about pore pressure.
 Water in the spaces between the grains,
 holding them apart,
 carrying load the grains would otherwise carry.
 Squeeze a fan like that and the water takes the strain and the grains ride on it,
 and the mud is strong right up until the moment the water finds somewhere to go.
 And it does not find anywhere quickly,
 because water moves through glacial flour about as fast as it moves through a bar of soap,
 and a failure surface in that material is a layer you could span with one hand,
 and what is in that layer does not equalise in an afternoon.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
